\f0\fs48 \cf2 Around 1668, Mariotte (who helped found the French Academy of Sciences) reported on the blind spot.  He thought that the choroid of the eye (the middle of the eye's three layers) was the light-sensitive part, because the choroid layer halted at the margin of the optic nerve head.  An understandable error, particularly because the detail of the retina (the third, innermost layer) wasn't understood for another two centuries.\
\
Why is there a blindspot?\
\
Working together, your eyes allow you to see across a wide space of the world (around 160 degrees wide).   \
\
Each eye, however, has a small space where it sees nothing:  this occurs because there must be an opening (the Optic Nerve Head) to allow neurons from INSIDE the eye to LEAVE THE EYE and reach the brain.  Because there are no photoreceptors at the Optic Nerve Head, there is a blind spot for each eye.  On average, this blind area is around 5 degrees wide, and lies about 15 degrees to the right of the straight-ahead position (for the right eye) and 15 degrees to the left of the straight-ahead position for the left eye.  \
\
Under normal conditions, you are unaware of this non-seeing area, because the vision of each eye covers the non-seeing area of the other eye.  \
\
When you are looking with one eye alone, however, the brain fills in the missing area with detail from the rest of the image in the eye.  Now you can measure and experience your own blindspot. \
\
